<!--META-DATA
{
	"tags": ["monte-carlo", "pi", "estimation", "simulation", "function"]
}
-->

<statement>
	<p>
		This function uses Monte Carlo simulation to estimate the value of <m>\pi</m>.
	</p>

	<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
		<col width="12%"/>
		<col width="88%" halign="left"/>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Inputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>N</pf> (1x1) integer — number of experiment trials. Default: <pf>1e5</pf></p>
				<p><pf>seed</pf> (1x1) integer — optional rng seed for reproducibility.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Outputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>
					<pf>simResults</pf> (1x1) struct containing the fields:
					<ul>
						<li><pf>N</pf> (1x1) integer — number of experiment trials.</li>
						<li><pf>CL</pf> (1x1) double — confidence level between 0-1.</li>
						<li><pf>ME</pf> (1x1) double — confidence interval margin of error.</li>
						<li><pf>approxPi</pf> (1x1) double — Monte Carlo estimate of <m>\pi</m></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Details:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ This simulation can be approached very similarly to the in-class demonstration where we approximated the area of a circle of radius r. Think about the connection of <m>\pi</m> to this problem.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell>
				<p>
					▸ Handle the special inputs options as follows:
					<ul marker="square">
						<li>If <pf>seed</pf> is provided, set the random number generator to this seed using the command: <pf>rng(seed,'twister')</pf></li>
						<li>If no inputs are provided, set <pf>N=1e5</pf> (shorthand for <m>10^5</m>).</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
	</tabular>

	<p>
		Examples:
		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 1: Estimate pi using 10,000 random points
				approx_pi = mc_approx_pi(10000)
				% returns a value near 3.14
			</code>
		</program>

		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 2: Reproducible estimate with fixed seed
				approx_pi = mc_approx_pi(1e6, 1234)
				% returns approximately 3.1417
			</code>
		</program>
	</p>
</statement>
